<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#parakoimomenos">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parakoimomenos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as parakoimōmenos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The parakoimōmenos, Greek for the one who sleeps beside the emperor's chamber, was a Byzantine court position usually reserved for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. Probably created no later than the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iv-the-khazar">Leo IV the Khazar</a> (775–780), it began as a modest post for chamber servants who slept outside the emperor's room as a security measure, but from the mid-9th century it became the highest post for eunuchs; holders such as Samonas, Joseph Bringas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-lekapenos">Basil Lekapenos</a> acted as chief ministers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil the Macedonian</a> used it to usurp the throne. It declined in the 12th century, was divided in two under the Palaiologoi, and by the 14th century was essentially an honorific dignity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eunuchs had a separate set of titles and could rise very high precisely because they could never become emperor: in the 9th and 10th centuries the <b>parakoimomenos</b>, the eunuch chamberlain who slept in the emperor’s bedchamber, often acted as chief minister.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
